  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This open-access scholarly book is drawing clear early use, with 2,132 recorded downloads, and it has a visible public-knowledge footprint through mentions in Wikipedia-linked and Crossref-connected sources. Its record also points to relevance beyond scholarship: the book aligns with gender equality and has been funded through KU Leuven support.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The Legacy of Elise Hall is a 2024 scholarly book by Kurt Bertels and Adrianne Honnold that revisits a neglected figure in music history and the broader cultural history of the saxophone. The book\u2019s subject matter sits at the intersection of narrative, identity in music, gender studies, and performing arts, and its reception record suggests that this framing is resonating with readers and discoverability systems alike. The book is openly available, which likely supports its use and circulation across scholarly and public audiences.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is usage: the book has been downloaded 2,132 times through OAPEN. That level of access indicates active reading and consultation, even though citation-based uptake is still at an early stage in the record. OpenAlex currently shows 0 citations, so the book\u2019s scholarly influence cannot yet be described in citation terms; instead, the evidence points to emerging visibility and readership rather than established citation traction.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book also has a modest but meaningful public-knowledge presence. The record shows mentions from Wikipedia-related and Crossref-connected sources, suggesting that the work is entering broader reference and discovery environments. At the same time, the public-discourse data are limited, so the safest reading is that the book has begun to circulate beyond a purely specialist context, but the available evidence is still thin.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Its thematic alignment with gender equality is especially fitting for a study centered on Elise Hall and the history of women\u2019s participation in musical performance. That alignment strengthens the book\u2019s relevance to conversations about representation and cultural memory. Combined with open access and support from KU Leuven, the record presents a book with clear public-facing reach and strong conceptual relevance, even as formal scholarly citation impact remains in its early phase.</p>",
